WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.475
Reaching orbit takes
speed as well as height.

2
00:00:03.775 --> 00:00:05.433
What if some of the machinery

3
00:00:05.433 --> 00:00:08.196
that gives a payload that
speed could stay in space?

4
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.063
A skyhook is a rotating tether in orbit.

5
00:00:13.363 --> 00:00:15.500
Near the bottom of its swing,

6
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.994
its tip moves against the orbital motion,

7
00:00:17.994 --> 00:00:21.200
reducing the speed an approaching
payload needs to match.

8
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.982
A launch vehicle climbs
to the meeting point.

9
00:00:27.282 --> 00:00:31.427
Position, speed and timing must align.

10
00:00:31.727 --> 00:00:35.323
A grapple secures the payload,
and the tether carries the load.

11
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:42.194
As the tether turns, the
payload gains orbital energy.

12
00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:48.035
At a chosen release point, it
leaves on its own trajectory.

13
00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:52.576
That energy comes from
the tether's motion.

14
00:00:52.876 --> 00:00:56.219
Before the next handoff,
the system must recover.

15
00:00:56.519 --> 00:00:59.216
Propulsion, or carefully
planned returning cargo,

16
00:00:59.216 --> 00:01:02.813
could help restore what
the outgoing payload took.

17
00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:07.430
One tether could become
part of a transport network:

18
00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:09.865
a lunar lunavator, an anchor at Phobos,

19
00:01:09.865 --> 00:01:12.299
and routes further into the solar system.

20
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:17.612
Skyhook Industries
explores that possibility.

21
00:01:17.912 --> 00:01:21.581
Design and test a flight in Flight Studio.

22
00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:25.058
Build and supply a network in Expeditions.

23
00:01:25.358 --> 00:01:27.236
Start with one handoff.

24
00:01:27.536 --> 00:01:28.882
See where it could lead.
